(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-nodules-neoplasms-quiz"]={"title":"Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which test in the lung cancer workup is noted to be not sensitive?","choices":["Bronchoscopic biopsy","Sputum cytology","Contrast computed tomography","Positron emission tomography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sputum cytology is listed among the additional tests but is not sensitive, so it is not relied on to find lung cancer. The exception is squamous cell carcinoma in the central bronchi, which, unlike the other types, can be caught with sputum testing. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 17, 30, 40"},{"q":"Which person without symptoms meets every lung cancer screening criterion?","choices":["Age 62, 40 pack-years, smokes now","Age 62, 40 pack-years, quit 20 years ago","Age 45, 40 pack-years, smokes now","Age 84, 40 pack-years, smokes now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening is for adults without symptoms aged 55 to 80 with at least a 30 pack-year history who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. A current smoker in their early sixties with 40 pack-years meets every part and is screened every year with low-dose chest computed tomography. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which laboratory test in the lung cancer workup would reveal hypercalcemia?","choices":["Pulmonary function tests","Comprehensive metabolic panel","Complete blood count","Alkaline phosphatase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lab workup lists a complete blood count with differential, a comprehensive metabolic panel including calcium, and an alkaline phosphatase. The calcium on the metabolic panel is how hypercalcemia shows up, a paraneoplastic finding linked particularly to squamous cell carcinoma. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 46"},{"q":"When a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm is evaluated, which result carries a recommendation for referral to a subspecialist such as a pulmonologist or thoracic surgeon?","choices":["Negative or mild uptake","Nodule smaller than 8 mm","Moderate or intense uptake","Very low probability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Referral to a subspecialist such as a pulmonologist, thoracic surgeon, interventional radiologist or oncologist is recommended for moderate or intense uptake on positron emission tomography, and for a high clinical probability of malignancy. Those nodules go on to biopsy or resection. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 12"},{"q":"In lung disease, carcinoid syndrome comes mainly from which tumor?","choices":["Bronchial carcinoid","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For lung causes, carcinoid syndrome comes mainly from a bronchial carcinoid, one of the rarer lung tumor types. Release of serotonin from the tumor cells causes flushing, apparent cyanosis, wheezing, diarrhea, cramps, hepatomegaly and thickening of the pulmonic and tricuspid valves. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 31, 46, 48"},{"q":"Why is large cell carcinoma a diagnosis of exclusion among non-small cell lung cancers?","choices":["It stains for mucin","It is found in sputum","Its cells are crushed","It lacks specific markers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma lacks specific genetic or histology markers, so it is diagnosed by exclusion among the non-small cell types. It is anaplastic, undifferentiated and pleomorphic, which separates it from adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, and histology must confirm the anaplastic tissue. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 40, 42"},{"q":"What does large cell carcinoma typically show on chest computed tomography?","choices":["Large peripheral cavitated mass","Central obstructing bronchial mass","Popcorn-calcified nodule","Smooth nodule with fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma shows a large, cavitated mass on chest computed tomography, typically in the periphery rather than centrally like squamous cell carcinoma, often with angiogenesis, its own blood supply. Biopsy with histology is still needed to confirm the anaplastic tissue. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21, 33, 34, 39"},{"q":"Which central lung cancer is usually metastatic by the time it is diagnosed?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is central, infiltrates the mucosa and develops insidiously, so over 70% of patients are already metastatic to the liver, bone or brain before detection. That is why it is usually metastatic by the time of diagnosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 33, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"Which signs appear later in adenocarcinoma, after the respiratory symptoms?","choices":["Cough and dyspnea","Hemoptysis and chest pain","Fatigue and chest tightness","Bone pain and weight loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenocarcinoma is mostly asymptomatic early, then usually presents with respiratory symptoms first: cough, shortness of breath, chest tightness or pain, hemoptysis and fatigue. Bone pain, weight loss and other systemic, paraneoplastic issues are the later signs. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the five-year survival for lung cancer in the United States?","choices":["About 5%","About 17%","About 30%","About 60%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer has one of the lowest five-year survival prognoses: about 17 to 18% of patients in the United States are alive five years after diagnosis, and the outlook is even worse in the rest of the world. Much of the reason is that symptoms appear only once the disease is advanced or already metastatic. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 12"},{"q":"Which finding sends a patient to a diagnostic workup for lung cancer instead of routine screening?","choices":["A 40 pack-year history","Current smoking","Radon exposure at home","Hemoptysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening is meant for high-risk people without symptoms or a known nodule. Hemoptysis is one of the presenting symptoms, along with persistent cough, dysphagia, wheezing or shortness of breath and systemic symptoms, that call for a workup for possible lung cancer instead. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 12, 18"},{"q":"Popcorn calcification in a lung nodule points to which lesion?","choices":["Healed granuloma","Adenocarcinoma","Carcinoid tumor","Hamartoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benign nodules tend to have well-defined borders and may contain fatty tissue or calcifications; popcorn-looking calcification means a hamartoma, a connective tissue growth. On computed tomography a hamartoma is a smoothly marginated solid nodule with internal fat and calcification. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 12, 16, 21, 32, 48"},{"q":"A 4.5 cm lung cancer has not spread to any lymph nodes or distant sites; which stage is it?","choices":["Stage 1B","Stage 2A","Stage 2B","Stage 3A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage 2A is a tumor more than 4 cm but no more than 5 cm in its greatest dimension that has not spread to any lymph nodes. A 4.5 cm node-negative tumor without metastasis fits exactly, T2b N0 M0. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 26"},{"q":"Biopsy confirms lung cancer in a patient who is fit for surgery but unfit for lobectomy; which treatment is used?","choices":["Radiofrequency ablation","Anatomical segmentectomy","Pneumonectomy","Completion lobectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a patient fit for surgery, image-guided biopsy that confirms malignancy leads to lobectomy, or to anatomical segmentectomy when the patient is unfit for lobectomy. A segment resection removes a larger portion of a lobe than a wedge resection but less than the entire lobe. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 35"},{"q":"Why does high uptake on positron emission tomography not prove that a nodule is malignant?","choices":["Tumor cells avoid the tracer","Uptake reflects nodule size","Inflammation can take it up","The tracer is not a sugar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Positron emission tomography uses a radioactive sugar, fludeoxyglucose, that highly metabolic cells take up, suggesting malignancy. Inflammatory conditions can also show high uptake, so uptake does not rule them out and tissue is still needed. Low or normal uptake is a good sign. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 22"},{"q":"Which cells lining the bronchioles give rise to the hormone-secreting paraneoplastic symptoms of small cell carcinoma?","choices":["Type I pneumocytes","Type II pneumocytes","Capillary lining cells","Neuroendocrine cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neuroendocrine cells are among the cells lining the bronchioles. When small cell carcinoma develops from these cells, which secrete polypeptide hormones, it produces neuroendocrine paraneoplastic symptoms, such as excess antidiuretic hormone with hyponatremia. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 41, 48"},{"q":"Papilledema on fundoscopic examination in a patient with lung cancer suggests metastasis to which organ?","choices":["The brain","The liver","The adrenal glands","The spine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Brain metastasis causes headaches, vision changes, nausea, vomiting, syncopal episodes and seizures. Raised intracranial pressure adds papilledema and other changes on fundoscopic examination, and a lesion near the midbrain or pons can affect the cranial nerves that move the eyes. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How do packs smoked per day and years of smoking affect lung cancer risk?","choices":["They compound the risk","Packs matter, years do not","Risk plateaus after ten years","Quitting erases the risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Packs per day and years of smoking compound the risk, which climbs from five-fold to ten-fold to twenty-fold and beyond as exposure builds. That is why the screening criterion is written as a pack-year history, a measure that combines the two. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 18"},{"q":"In what share of lung cancer cases is smoking implicated?","choices":["About 20%","About 50%","85 to 90%","Nearly 100%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is implicated in 85 to 90% of lung cancer cases, with second-hand smoke listed at 20%, and packs per day and years of smoking compound the risk. Most patients are over 50 with a smoking history, though smoking is not always associated with lung cancer. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 31"},{"q":"Which lymph node involvement automatically moves lung cancer to at least stage 3?","choices":["Hilar nodes on the tumor side","Intrapulmonary nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Peribronchial nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. N2 or N3 disease, mediastinal or contralateral lymph involvement, automatically moves lung cancer to stage 3. N2 is same-side mediastinal or subcarinal nodes; N3 is contralateral mediastinal or hilar nodes, or any scalene or supraclavicular node. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 24, 25, 26"},{"q":"Which nodule appearance is concerning for malignancy?","choices":["Internal fat","Spiculated border","Well-defined border","Popcorn calcification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A spiculated appearance, with spikes, deviations or lobulations in the border, is one of the concerning nodule features; the invasive adenocarcinoma example is a suspicious solid spiculated nodule. Other concerning features include size over 6 mm, upper lobe or hilar location and growth over time. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21, 32"},{"q":"What is the standard management of stage 1 or 2 non-small cell lung cancer?","choices":["Radiation alone","Immunotherapy alone","Surgical resection","Chemotherapy alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Standard management for stages 1A through 2B is surgical resection, chosen by size and lymph involvement: a wedge or segment resection or a lobectomy, open or thoracoscopic, or a pneumonectomy. The patient must be able to tolerate surgery, meaning survive it and recover. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 35, 37, 38"},{"q":"Which is a chronic effect of lung radiotherapy?","choices":["Skin erythema","Acute inflammation","Marked vomiting","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic effects of lung radiotherapy include pulmonary fibrosis and scarring of the treated tissue, cardiovascular fibrosis with coronary disease, valve disease or cardiomyopathy, localized hair loss, lymphedema from lymphatic damage, and neuropathy. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What makes lung cancer one of the more deadly cancers?","choices":["Symptoms appear late","Most nodules are malignant","It cannot be biopsied","It rarely spreads"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer is more deadly than many cancers because patients do not present with symptoms until the disease is advanced or already metastatic. That late presentation helps explain a five-year survival of 17 to 18% in the United States and the case for screening high-risk people without symptoms. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 12, 14, 41"},{"q":"Which paraneoplastic finding is linked particularly to squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Hyponatremia","Cutaneous flushing","Progressive leg weakness","Hypercalcemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Paraneoplastic syndromes come from secreted hormonal or cytokine effects of tumor tissue, and hypercalcemia is the endocrine syndrome linked particularly to squamous cell carcinoma. Calcium is measured on the comprehensive metabolic panel in the lab workup. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 46, 47, 48"},{"q":"Who is the lung cancer screening recommendation meant for?","choices":["Patients with a known nodule","Patients with hemoptysis","High-risk adults without symptoms","Anyone who has ever smoked"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The United States Preventive Services Task Force recommends screening for certain high-risk individuals without symptoms or a known nodule, a benefit shown in the National Lung Cancer Screening Trial. High risk means the age band plus a heavy pack-year history in someone who smokes now or quit within the last 15 years. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18"},{"q":"A 2 cm lung cancer has spread to mediastinal lymph nodes on the same side, with no distant metastasis; which stage is it?","choices":["Stage 2B","Stage 3A","Stage 3B","Stage 3C"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A tumor no more than 5 cm that has spread to the mediastinal lymph nodes, subcarinal nodes included, on the side of the tumor is stage 3A. Mediastinal involvement, N2, automatically moves a cancer to stage 3, whatever the size of the tumor. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28"},{"q":"Which lung cancer has the strongest association with smoking?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma, the second most common lung cancer, has the strongest association with smoking. It is central, often arises in a bronchus and shows frequent cavitation and obstruction, with cough, wheezing and hemoptysis once the bronchus is blocked. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 33, 39, 41"},{"q":"A 2.5 cm lung cancer has not spread to any lymph nodes or distant sites; which stage is it?","choices":["Stage 1A","Stage 1B","Stage 2A","Stage 2B"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1A is a tumor no more than 3 cm in its greatest dimension that has not spread to any lymph nodes. At 2.5 cm with no nodes and no metastasis, this tumor is T1 N0 M0; all stage 1 to 3 tumors are free of distant metastasis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 25"},{"q":"After a response to initial therapy, which lung cancer is offered prophylactic cranial irradiation?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In small cell carcinoma with a complete or partial response to initial therapy, prophylactic cranial irradiation is recommended in limited stage and considered, with or without thoracic radiotherapy, in extensive stage. Brain magnetic resonance imaging is the preferred scan of the brain. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 40, 43"}]};
